Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP/RTU client for reading/writing holding registers to field devices
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus returns response objects with an isError() method and .registers list; pyModbusTCP typically returns a plain list of register 
    │   values or None on failure, requiring rewritten error handling; modbus-tk uses a master.execute(slave_id, function_code, ...) call pattern rather than 
    │   typed methods like read_holding_registers/write_register; minimalmodbus is primarily designed for serial (RS-485/RS-232) Modbus RTU/ASCII, not native 
    │   TCP; using it for TCP requires additional socket-to-serial shims or a different transport layer entirely; the 'unit' keyword argument (slave/unit id) 
    │   is named and passed differently across these libraries (unit= vs slaveaddress vs slave id positional arg); connection lifecycle calls (connect/close) 
    │   differ: pymodbus uses explicit connect(), pyModbusTCP uses open(), minimalmodbus opens per-call or via serial port config
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All four packages implement Modbus master/client functionality capable of reading and writing holding registers over TCP (or serial), matching the 
        file's core need to read temperature/pressure and write valve position via Modbus. The docstring itself flags pymodbus->minimalmodbus as a substitution
        target, confirming the intended equivalence class, though the runtime APIs differ significantly.

